  {
    "episode_id": 66284,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/6/66284.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 4,
    "turn_text": "Well, we are seeing a reduced overall number of calls having to do with COVID-19 assistance. And I think that that is twofold. First of all, I think of the federal assistance, which is coming up to somewhere in the range of 20 billion or so to what you overall or more at this point actually did work, did stabilize people and businesses as much as possible during that very terrible, the heat of the pandemic. But also, you know, the economy is coming back. And so I think there's just a little bit more of a groove to things. We definitely still have calls. There's a lot of questions, for example, unemployment insurance right straight through was the very top concern of folks that were calling my office, which was frustrating because I'm not in charge of unemployment insurance, so that's a state issue. I could help them. And that is still a top issue, but we're starting to see a tapering off of questions of whether somebody got their direct payment or some other assistance. We're starting to see some related kinds of assistance requests. For example, we are starting to see people that were backed up in terms of processing passports or immigration or wanting to bring their families here. Those things are picking up again, but the direct COVID-19 related calls to my office are down by about half this year, the first half of this year. Of course, they're still up by a half over a year before that. So there's still a lot of need out there.",
