        "confidence": 0.88
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "The speaker believes the governor’s public statements carry significant weight and are newsworthy.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker assumes that the lifting of the moratorium will lead to real economic hardship for renters.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker trusts that the state’s announced program is credible and potentially effective.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker believes that the recipient organization is involved in national-level policy work related to housing stability.",
        "confidence": 0.86
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker views the issue of evictions as both urgent and systemic at the national level.",
        "confidence": 0.85
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Topic Shift"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 66284,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/6/66284.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 6,
    "turn_text": "Well, it absolutely is a concern. It's a very difficult situation. We've had an eviction moratorium in place. at the federal and the state level for 16, 17 months now. And I think that that was necessary. I think it was necessary not to kick people out of their homes if they couldn't pay their rent. I think that was the right policy call by both the federal government and the state. We did try to compensate landlords with assistance to landlords, whether it be PPP assistance or direct landlord related assistance. We tried to compensate. Renters for that matter, who if they could pay should be paying to their landlords. And so, you know, we tried to we tried to get ourselves through again, the economic consequences of COVID-19 to a place where we can start to pick up the economy again. but you simply can't continue full on eviction moratorium forever. And so the question really from a policy perspective is, well, okay, are enough people stabilized so that you can actually reinstate the normal rules, but have a good enough safety net that you will be able to help and still catch folks that are just not back to where they need to be. And at some point, you need to make the call that it's time to dip back in that direction. And I think there's probably is about the right time to do that. I think it's important to note also that although sometimes people think that you know landlords are all big corporations that that can absorb these kinds of losses of income that's actually not the case many if not most are small businesses or families and they have foregone their own income as they had their own mortgage obligations so this is a chain that that has to be unwound at some point and And I think we've got to go back in that direction, but we definitely need to make sure those safety nets are in place. The federal government is certainly helped with the aid that we have sent to the state and the county governments. And as we know, both the state and the county governments have with that aid set up their own assistance programs to take care of those that are particularly impacted. But yes, it is going to be a difficult time as we as we readjust.",
