  {
    "episode_id": 66284,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/6/66284.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 12,
    "turn_text": "Well, of course, the entire tourism industry was the most severely impacted industry in the entire country. In fact, the entire world because of COVID-19 for obvious reasons. And when we think of tourism, we forget sometimes that that's not just hotels. That's not just you know, the activities around hotels, that's everything that happens around tourism. That is our restaurants. Our restaurants rely primarily on our visitors. Of course, when we have local residents that are sitting home, that doesn't help things either. But also our entertainment venues, there are just our wedding operators. There's a host of folks that relate one way or the other, too. to the visitor industry. And in the beginning of COVID for the first year plus, we were focused primarily on general assistance, federal assistance to all small businesses, regardless of whether you are a restaurant or a steel mill or whatever it was, and trying to help folks out through a variety of programs, but it became evident that there were particular industries that were particularly hard hit. Airlines would be an example and restaurants was a definite example. And so in the American Rescue Plan that we passed earlier this year, we set up what we referred to as the Restaurant Revitalization Fund, which was targeted assistance to our restaurants, of which, as you've already noted, Hawaii restaurants were particularly hard hit. And we set that up at about $28 billion and we put it out there and there was tremendous, you know, the system was overwhelmed right away to the point that roughly, I forget what the figure was, but just hundreds of thousands of applications of which About half were actually funded before that program ran out of money. The same thing happened in Hawaii where we had about 2,000 restaurants that applied, only about 1,000 received assistance. And so it was clear that we needed to plus up that program. I'm part of an effort by a lot of the folks across the country have a similar situation to to add another $60 billion to that restaurant revitalization fund, which is actually the shortfall that we're facing right now and estimated demand on that program. So we're trying very hard to get more aid to our restaurants. I",
