  {
    "episode_id": 66284,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/6/66284.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 14,
    "turn_text": "Yes, I do agree with the governor. I think that the governor from the beginning of COVID-19 has placed the public health advice, the public health goals, the public health mandates first and foremost. And that is unlike many other parts of this country. in which people started to feel that somehow they could beat COVID-19 without those kinds of protections, and somehow if we just revitalized the economy without those protections, it would take care of COVID-19. And that was proved dead wrong. In fact, I think if you look back at our history in Hawaii, one would have to conclude that when we actually did relax our mandates in the summer of 2020, when everybody else was saying, oh, this is over, and we let our guard down, and we got nailed because of that. And so, and that was a decision that I think was perhaps not putting the strict public health side of it first, and trying to get back to a normal life, and COVID doesn't allow you to do that. It is a non-discriminatory disease. as we know, very adjustable. We have variants out there right now. And you cannot relax your guard on it. And so I don't really care if the rest of the country feels that somehow we're being too protective. I frankly think the rest of the country has let their guard down too soon. And we are seeing that now because we are seeing a resurgence of COVID-19 across a great part of our country, including parts that are nowhere near our vaccination level, which is the second best in the country, but even second best in the country. If you're not vaccinated from COVID-19, you're at total risk when you start to open up again, and that's what we see happening right now with triple digits in the last week. And so I think the governor is being cautious, and I think cautious is what you have to do.",
