  {
    "episode_id": 66284,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/6/66284.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 16,
    "turn_text": "I absolutely do think we have to consider this. This was building to a breaking point before COVID-19. You know when we got to 10 million visitors a year and went past 10 million visitors a year when when when we you know you know started to just you know see that visitors and every single aspect of our lives as opposed to being able to go back to our homes and and kind of you know have a home life without an illegal vacation rental next door and without a bunch of You know, to our helicopters flying overhead disturbing our piece of our neighborhood and when we, you know, couldn't get into our beaches to our to our trails when we had litter throughout Hawaii when. When the level of tourism got to a point, tourism in Hawaii, which has had a long and storied history, as we all know, has always depended on one thing, and that is the acceptance of residents, because people don't come to Hawaii just for our beauty. They come because they feel comfortable. They feel welcome. They feel that this is a safe place. And when you start to lose the support of the residents themselves, that gets around. And pretty soon visitors say, well, that's not a friendly place to go. Why should I go there? And so we have to be very, very realistic about this. What happened during COVID-19 was that a terrible pandemic gave us a glimpse of a Hawaii without tourism and we all liked it. And so we're reluctant to go back to a world in which we have 10 million plus tourists. Again, we have legal vacation rentals where we Where we facing all of these consequences. And so I think this is a very, very real discussion that we have to have that maybe a sea change in a in a three generation now. You know March towards you know highest volume tourism possible, and I think we got to have that discussion now sometimes I hear people say Ring their hands and at the state and county level and say well as us we can't do anything about this You know people get to come here if they want well, yes, it's true that people can Constitutionally for the most part fly to Hawaii if they want But you know if you've got you know, half of your hotel rooms or dwelling units that are illegal vacation rentals, and you haven't cracked down on your illegal vacation rental laws, well, then that's a no-brainer about how to go after this, because if you drive that up, then you will have higher hotel prices, but that will have the effective lower visitors, and this may seem like treason to some people, but I think this is the reality. You don't have to build another set of gates at the airport and increase airplane capacity. You can impose reasonable resident versus non-resident user fees at all of our attractions. So we have tools in our toolkits that in the state, you don't have to zone new hotels. You don't have to make it so easy to renovate old hotels to much higher density and room purposes. So there are things that we can do, and this is a discussion we need to have, because to go back to the, you know, as best lawyers say, the status quo ante before COVID-19, I don't think it's going to result in a good solid tourism industry for us going forward. We're going to have the kind of open revolts we've started to see against tourism.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
