  {
    "episode_id": 66284,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/6/66284.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 20,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think you're referring to the child tax credit. Under our federal tax laws, we provide many people throughout the country that have minor children with a tax credit to help them to raise those children. That tax credit depends a little bit on what your income is. Above a certain income, you're not eligible for the credit, but most people are eligible for the credit. It's up to $2,000 per child, a minor child. It was. In the emergency assistance that we passed for to respond to COVID-19, we increased that child tax credit to $3,600 max per child. This was for the current tax year. And we did that because we wanted to help families throughout the country. Families were struggling and that was another way. for us to be able to help families that needed help, especially families with children, especially families like so many who have to work two jobs in order to support themselves and their families and have to pay for things like child care, which as we all know is a cripplingly expensive in Hawaii. And so we increased it to 3,600, but we also did something else which hadn't been done before, and that is that we allowed for half of that credit to be advanced to people in a monthly payment. It used to be you had to kind of wait for the tax year to be over, and then you did a look back at your credit at tax time. Now we're advancing half of that. That just started yesterday. And I think that that was warranted. I think that that will help folks. And so if you think that you are in that category of folks that are eligible for that, and you have any questions whatsoever about whether you're getting it, it should come as a direct payment if you have paid your taxes in past years. If you're not kind of on record with the IRS already, then it's a little bit like the direct payments. We went through this. you know, long effort to make sure that we got payments to people in various ways from directly positive bank accounts to checks to sometimes it was a lot more complicated if people were, you know, not filing tax returns or whatever. But if you have any questions about this, that's one area that my office can definitely help with.",
