  {
    "episode_id": 66284,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/6/6/66284.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 22,
    "turn_text": "Well, certainly, like any program like this when the federal government changes a program or updates a program of education is everything. And the effort is to send out to as many people as possible to everybody exactly what we've done and why we've done it. I am absolutely sure that we're going to go through a period of months in which there are going to be many, many questions about this. And, you know, the fact of the matter is that the folks that need it most are often the folks that are least connected with government. And so that's on my shoulders and my colleagues here and my federal government is to pursue every effort we can to to educate people on the availability of this program, which was really no different than many of the programs that we had to roll out on an emergency basis over the last 17 months of COVID-19. So we're right in the middle of that right now. And just to make this point, there are people that legally don't file their tax returns. They don't have to file their tax returns. And so the question is, how do we, because they don't have They don't have the income that requires a tax return to be. to be filed. And so these are folks that many times are the ones that we need to reach the most. So there are many, many organizations out there that are trying to get the information out. Our nonprofits are being very, very good about this to many, many of our communities. But like I said, it's going to be a lift to get that word out. Nobody's going to lose out if they are eligible for it. They're not going to lose out if it takes a little bit of time to get the word. you know, the money is available right now if people are eligible and it's needed out there. As to whether to make it permanent, that's part of a much broader question that we are facing in Congress right now because we have in fact sent out somewhere in the range of $5 trillion of emergency assistance in the last 17 months. All of that needed, I believe, All of it borrowed. So we together with the fact that it was needed and we did borrow it. We worsened our federal budget about as severely as ever since the Second World War. And that has to be a consideration. We have several programs that were temporary emergency programs that people now want to be continued. And of course, some of them probably shouldn't be continued. From my perspective, the question that I have to ask is, well, what are the priorities to continue them? And then how do we pay for those? How do we not worsen our federal budget even more so? To extend the current increase in the child tax credit, 10 years would cost somewhere in the range of $600 billion. So if you take that against a normal federal budget of $1.5 trillion, which is what my appropriations committee has just been doing this week, That is a lot of borrowing and or money and or increased taxes or reduced revenues in order to pay for that. So it's not an easy policy choice. It may be the right thing to do. But there are trade-offs all over the place that we're having to deal with right now.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Assert / Elaborate"
  },
  {
